WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.120
Mental health, physical conditions, and other&nbsp;
personal struggles have caused some of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.720
favorite Hollywood stars to leave the industry.&nbsp;
It’s always heartbreaking to see them go, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.920
sometimes they are happier for it. Keep watching&nbsp;
to find out why these actors quit Hollywood.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:22.240
Many '90s kids will remember Amanda Bynes'&nbsp;
turn in Nickelodeon's "All That." Her zany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.720
attention-stealing energy was watchable&nbsp;
enough to land her a sketch show of her own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.360
fittingly called "The Amanda Show," and&nbsp;
a lead role on "What I Like About You."

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.960
From there, she transitioned to film, picking&nbsp;
up lead roles in "What a Girl Wants" in 2003&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.480
and the teen Shakespeare flick "She's the&nbsp;
Man" in 2006. She also appeared as part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:49.280
the ensemble cast of "Hairspray" in 2007, which&nbsp;
made a tidy profit at the box office. In 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.800
she played the gossipy, moralizing mean girl&nbsp;
in "Easy A" opposite Emma Stone — another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.600
well-received teen literary adaptation. Despite&nbsp;
these apparent successes, Bynes tweeted in 2010,

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.400
"If I don't love something&nbsp;
anymore I stop doing it. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.200
don't love acting anymore&nbsp;
so I've stopped doing it."

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.840
Just a month later, Bynes&nbsp;
tweeted that she had, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.120
"unretired." She then deleted her Twitter account&nbsp;
altogether, only to get a new one. These were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.760
early signs of what became a long and highly&nbsp;
publicized series of mental health episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.600
including a string of DUIs,&nbsp;
hit-and-runs, and an arrest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.680
She also accused her father of abuse only to&nbsp;
recant this claim in a bizarre tweet. She wrote,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.640
"My dad never did any of those things ... The&nbsp;
microchip in my brain made me say those things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.080
but he's the one that ordered&nbsp;
them to microchip me."

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.120
She threatened a lot of&nbsp;
people with a lot of lawsuits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.280
reserving particular fire for any tabloid&nbsp;
that claimed she was misusing drugs.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.760
Finally, in 2013, Bynes entered rehab.&nbsp;
In 2014, she tweeted that she had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.120
diagnosed with bipolar disorder and manic&nbsp;
depression and that she was in treatment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.440
These days, she seems to be on the mend.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.680
Although "Easy A" remains her last role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:07.040
she revealed in a 2017 interview that she was&nbsp;
three years sober and wanted to act again.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:14.480
A hot commodity in the 1990s and 2000s, Cameron&nbsp;
Diaz had a very successful career, earning enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:20.480
money to lead several lifetimes of luxury. From&nbsp;
her first acting experience in 1994's "The Mask"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.640
to her starring turn in "There's Something&nbsp;
About Mary," and from Spike Jonze's "Being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:31.200
John Malkovich" to Martin Scorsese's "Gangs of New&nbsp;
York," her filmography shows considerable range.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.400
Yet creative block happens to the best of us. This&nbsp;
is why Diaz hasn't appeared in a film since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:43.120
2014 musical comedy remake of "Annie." Speaking&nbsp;
at a summit hosted by Gwyneth Paltrow, Diaz said,

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.560
"I just went, 'I can't really&nbsp;
say who I am to myself,'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.800
which is a hard thing to face up to.&nbsp;
I felt the need to make myself whole."

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.200
She has few regrets about retiring and&nbsp;
has kept busy over the last seven years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.960
In 2015, Diaz married Good&nbsp;
Charlotte lead guitarist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.640
Benji Madden, and four years later,&nbsp;
their daughter Raddix was born.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.960
“I love him so much it hurts.”

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:12.000
Before that, in 2016, Diaz published her book,&nbsp;
"The Longevity Book: The Science of Aging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.560
the Biology of Strength, and the Privilege of&nbsp;
Time." It was followed by 2013's "The Body Book:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.840
Feed, Move, Understand and&nbsp;
Love Your Amazing Body."

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.320
Christopher Reeve had a thriving&nbsp;
career. A graduate of Juilliard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.120
he had his breakout role in the original&nbsp;
"Superman" movie from 1978 and continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.960
to work on several other projects, including&nbsp;
"The Remains of the Day" in 1993 and many plays.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:43.280
In 1995, Reeve attended a dressage and&nbsp;
show jumping event in Culpepper, Virginia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:49.040
He'd learned to ride in 1985 for his performance&nbsp;
as Count Vronsky in "Anna Karenina" and fell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.480
in love with the sport, developing a close bond&nbsp;
with Eastern Express, his American thoroughbred.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.120
Tragically, Eastern Express suddenly balked&nbsp;
on the third jump in the final competition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.440
sending Reeve over the saddle and on&nbsp;
to his head, crushing his first and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.080
second vertebrae. This devastating injury&nbsp;
paralyzed the actor from the neck down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.640
causing him to use a wheelchair and ventilator&nbsp;
for the rest of his life. According to one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.760
Reeve's physicians, the actor would have died&nbsp;
instantly had he fallen 1 centimeter to the left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.360
and experienced only a concussion had&nbsp;
he fallen 1 centimeter to the right.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.480
Reeve was not a quitter, though. He pulled&nbsp;
through intense grief and suicidal thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.920
to lead a busy life. Reeve campaigned for research&nbsp;
into spinal injuries and stem cell therapy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.640
raising some $138 million for&nbsp;
researchers around the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.040
He forayed into directing and had a few&nbsp;
appearances in shows like the TV remake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:46.080
of "Rear Window" and the CW's "Smallville,"&nbsp;
but his acting experience largely ended with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.160
the accident. After a period of ill health,&nbsp;
he died of heart failure in 2004 at age 52.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.200
In 1985, Michael J. Fox became one&nbsp;
of Hollywood's hottest young actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.680
with the role of Marty McFly in "Back&nbsp;
to the Future" — a rip-roaring sci-fi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.760
comedy that topped the box office and&nbsp;
became a classic of American cinema.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:12.320
Fox reprised McFly in two sequels while continuing&nbsp;
his run on the hit NBC sitcom "Family Ties."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:17.040
He also starred in "Teen Wolf," "Bright&nbsp;
Lights, Big City," and "Casualties of War."

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.640
It was during the production of "Doc Hollywood"&nbsp;
in 1991 that Fox noticed his finger was twitching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:27.760
and that he had a creeping pain in his shoulder.&nbsp;
These were symptoms of early-onset Parkinson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.040
disease — a shocking revelation, since most&nbsp;
people who get the condition are over 60.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.440
Doctors told the 29-year-old star that he&nbsp;
would have to quit acting in just a few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.560
but he proved them wrong with a&nbsp;
busy career that included roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.360
in "Mars Attacks" and "Stuart Little." His&nbsp;
biggest success was the Emmy-winning sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:51.040
"Spin City," which saw him play Deputy Mayor of&nbsp;
New York City Mike Flaherty for 103 episodes.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.320
“Oh, uh, I-I-I completely&nbsp;
forgot. We-we’ll just wing it.”

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.880
Fox retired from the show in 2000&nbsp;
because of his worsening condition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:05.600
Regular TV work followed in the 2000s and 2010s,&nbsp;
including turns in "Curb Your Enthusiasm," "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.640
Good Wife," and "The Michael J. Fox Show"&nbsp;
— a sitcom inspired by the actor's life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.880
Sadly, he wrote in his 2020 memoir that&nbsp;
he was entering a “second retirement”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.320
because of new symptoms including&nbsp;
delusions, memory loss, and dementia.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:26.000
Shelley Duvall was once one of Hollywood's most&nbsp;
recognizable faces. She rose to fame in the 1970s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.840
for her collaborations with celebrated director&nbsp;
Robert Altman, including "Brewster McCloud,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:37.600
"McCabe &amp; Mrs. Miller," "Nashville," and "3&nbsp;
Women." Nearly all of these are now considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:43.040
must-watch classics. Her success continued into&nbsp;
the ‘80s with such films as Stanley Kubrick's "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:49.200
Shining" in 1980, Robert Altman’s "Popeye"&nbsp;
from the same year, and "Roxanne" in 1987.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:55.840
After TV appearances and supporting roles&nbsp;
in the 1990s, Duvall quit Hollywood in 2002,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.680
leading a reclusive life with her&nbsp;
husband in her home state of Texas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.360
In 2016, she gave an interview on "Dr. Phil"&nbsp;
that raised serious questions about her mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.400
health. Disheveled and barely recognizable,&nbsp;
Duvall made a series of bizarre claims,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:16.000
like insisting Robin Williams was still alive&nbsp;
and that he'd become a 'shapeshifter,' saying,

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.760
"He looks real good in some&nbsp;
forms, in other forms he doesn't."

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:20.720
“Do you see him?”

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.720
“Have, yes.”
Many commentators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:28.480
derided the interview, calling it, quote,&nbsp;
"craven" and "sensational." In January 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.760
Seth Abramovitch of The Hollywood Reporter&nbsp;
managed to locate Duvall in Texas Hill Country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:38.560
and interview her. Happily, Abramovitch&nbsp;
found that Duvall possessed a sharp memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:43.040
full of engrossing stories. In the&nbsp;
nuanced, reflective piece, he noted,

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.640
"[While she can be] gripped by&nbsp;
anxiety attacks or meander into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:51.360
unsettling descriptions of alien-surveillance&nbsp;
programs, [she can also] converse for long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.360
coherent stretches and conjure up the&nbsp;
slightest details about her life."

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.480
Abramovitch speculated that Duvall fled to Texas&nbsp;
for both mental health and financial reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:05.200
Although it's sad that we won't see her&nbsp;
considerable talents onscreen, it is reassuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:09.680
to know that the "Dr. Phil" interview was a&nbsp;
tabloid misrepresentation of her mental health.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.480
Mara Wilson became one of the biggest child&nbsp;
stars in Hollywood after her performances in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:19.920
"Mrs. Doubtfire" and "Matilda." However, when&nbsp;
Wilson reached puberty in the early 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.640
the roles suddenly dried up. When she did get&nbsp;
auditions, she realized she was getting typecast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:30.000
as a, quote, "fat girl" stock character.&nbsp;
Speaking with People magazine, Wilson said,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.160
"I realized, 'I don't fit their idea&nbsp;
of what a Hollywood actress looks like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:35.920
so there's no room for me here.'"

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.280
She also said,

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.120
“[Sometimes I] wished to be in an&nbsp;
accident where I’d injure my nose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:44.080
and jaw so I could get reconstruction guilt-free."

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.440
Wilson decided to focus on schooling&nbsp;
rather than her acting career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:52.160
eventually attending New York University. Sadly,&nbsp;
the pressures of Hollywood stayed with her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:57.440
especially when she saw actresses her age like&nbsp;
Scarlett Johansson and Kristen Stewart on magazine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:02.560
covers. Fortunately, Wilson was able to manage&nbsp;
these anxieties through writing. These days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:06.800
she lives in New York as a comedy writer,&nbsp;
voice actress, and a notable Twitter persona.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:14.800
Tim Curry has had a long and busy career, logging&nbsp;
some 236 credits on IMDb. This eclectic body of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:20.000
work includes "The Rocky Horror Picture Show,"&nbsp;
"IT," "The Hunt for Red October," "Clue," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:25.360
"Congo," as well as dozens of TV appearances and&nbsp;
Broadway shows. The British actor was working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:31.440
around six projects a year when he suffered&nbsp;
a debilitating stroke in 2012, which affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.720
his speech and caused him to use a wheelchair.&nbsp;
Since then, Curry has only done voice acting. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:43.120
has credited his sense of humor as being, quote,&nbsp;
"absolutely vital" to his recovery. He also said,

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:46.800
"It's not tough to maintain,&nbsp;
it is just part of my DNA."

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:50.240
“Ah, you’re just in time for your party.”

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:54.960
He made one of his first public appearances since&nbsp;
his stroke to accept a lifetime achievement award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:59.600
at the 19th Actors Fund Tony&nbsp;
Awards Viewing Party in 2015.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:02.560
He was very grateful for the award, explaining,

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.440
"[It] solidifies the kind of work the American&nbsp;
acting community has given me for years now...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:10.240
I was thrilled when they told&nbsp;
me and I am thrilled now."

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:15.280
Sometimes, the critics really do hurt.&nbsp;
This was the case for Greta Garbo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:21.440
whose 1941 film "Two-Faced Woman" was so&nbsp;
maligned that the iconic actress left Tinseltown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:26.880
She was 36 years old and had made 31 movies&nbsp;
in just 20 years, which reflected not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:32.160
her work ethic but also her enormous popularity&nbsp;
in both the U.S. and her native Europe. She had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.960
even made the transition from silent films to&nbsp;
talkies — a rare feat among her contemporaries.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:42.720
Yet Garbo found little satisfaction in her career,&nbsp;
preferring the solitude she pursued from 1941&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:49.600
until her death in 1990 at the age of 85. She&nbsp;
was almost persuaded to return on two occasions:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:54.880
first an offer to play French novelist George&nbsp;
Sand, and then a lead role in Alfred Hitchcock's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:59.760
"The Paradine Case." Ultimately, she decided&nbsp;
to stick to her private, drifting lifestyle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:02.880
She wrote in a letter to her&nbsp;
friend Martha Wachtmeister,

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:06.720
"I am almost always alone and talk&nbsp;
to myself. I drive to the beach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:10.320
and take walks and that's always&nbsp;
marvelous. But that's it."

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:11.440
“I want to be alone.”

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:17.280
Frances Farmer had dreams of a theater career when&nbsp;
she signed a seven-year contract with Paramount in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:23.280
the mid-1930s. Under this contract, she performed&nbsp;
with Bing Crosby in "Rhythm on the Range,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:28.240
Cary Grant in "The Toast of New York,"&nbsp;
and Walter Brennan in "Come and Get It."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.480
Still craving success on the stage,&nbsp;
Farmer joined big Broadway productions.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:36.080
She was splitting her time between New&nbsp;
York and Los Angeles when her mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:41.360
collapsed in 1942 — a year that brought&nbsp;
her divorce, drunken traffic violations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:47.120
and the cancellation of her contract. Farmer spent&nbsp;
the rest of the 1940s in and out of hospitals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.720
sanitariums. She claimed she was abused at these&nbsp;
institutions in her posthumous autobiography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:55.920
though there’s some speculation that&nbsp;
the book may have been embellished.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:59.760
After a comeback appearance on&nbsp;
"This Is Your Life" in 1958,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:04.720
Farmer had several television parts and a role&nbsp;
in one film, "The Party Crashers." This would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:11.680
one of her last on-screen performances. Farmer&nbsp;
died of esophageal cancer in 1970 at age 56.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:16.400
Partly because of her radical-leaning politics in&nbsp;
her youth and partly because of her tragic life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:21.360
Farmer became something of a cult&nbsp;
figure. Even Kurt Cobain was a fan.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:26.000
If you or someone you know is struggling with&nbsp;
mental health, please contact the Crisis Text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:32.880
Line by texting HOME to 741741, call the&nbsp;
National Alliance on Mental Illness helpline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:43.760
at 1-800-950-NAMI (6264), or visit the&nbsp;
National Institute of Mental Health website.

